---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-scandinavia`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Scandinavia',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Scandinavia', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Scandinavia: Cod Liver Oil, NNR 2023, Polar Night, and Migrant Deficiency"
  description="Nordic vitamin D — traditional cod liver oil, NNR 2023 recommendations, polar-night deficiency in Norway/Sweden/Finland, and severe migrant-population rates."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-scandinavia", label: "Vitamin D in Scandinavia" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Scandinavia</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Scandinavia — Norway, Sweden, Denmark, Finland, Iceland — sits between
      55° and 70° N. Vitamin D winter runs 6–7 months in the south and stretches
      to include polar night in the far north. Traditional Scandinavian diets
      (oily fish, cod liver oil) preserved vitamin D status for centuries;
      modern populations rely on supplementation and fortification.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Nordic Nutrition Recommendations 2023</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>All adults:</strong> 10 µg/day (400 IU).</li>
      <li><strong>Age 75+ and pregnant/breastfeeding women:</strong> 20 µg/day (800 IU).</li>
      <li><strong>Children 1–17:</strong> 10 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Infants:</strong> 10 µg/day from age 4 weeks.</li>
      <li><strong>Upper limit:</strong> 100 µg/day adults, 50 µg/day children.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cod liver oil — the traditional supplement</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Norwegian and Swedish tradition of daily cod liver oil (tran / tran-olja)
      through winter is not folklore — it delivers 400–1,000 IU vitamin D per
      teaspoon plus omega-3 and vitamin A. Predates commercial supplements by
      centuries. Still widely used; Møller's Tran is the classic Norwegian brand.
      One-tablespoon-a-day of cod liver oil provides roughly 400–1,000 IU
      cholecalciferol depending on formulation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fortification</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Finland</strong> — mandatory fluid milk fortification (1 µg/100 mL) since 2003; dropped adult deficiency dramatically.</li>
      <li><strong>Sweden</strong> — fortified plant milks, low-fat dairy, spreads.</li>
      <li><strong>Norway</strong> — voluntary fortification of milk, spreads, breakfast cereals.</li>
      <li><strong>Denmark</strong> — no mandatory dairy fortification; higher deficiency than Finland.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Migrant population deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Somali, Afghan, Iraqi, and Syrian migrants to Scandinavia (dark skin, often
      conservative dress, cultural adaptation to lower outdoor time) show among
      the highest vitamin D deficiency rates recorded — 80–98% in some cohorts.
      Rickets has re-emerged as a paediatric concern in migrant communities.
      Nordic public health programmes provide free supplements to at-risk groups.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Polar night</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Above the Arctic Circle (66.5° N — Tromsø, Kiruna, Rovaniemi), the sun does
      not rise for weeks in mid-winter. Vitamin D synthesis is zero. Traditional
      Sami diet included substantial reindeer liver, marine mammal fat, and cod
      liver oil. Modern populations without those foods depend on supplementation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When Scandinavians can make vitamin D</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Roughly late April through mid-September in the south (Copenhagen, Stockholm,
      Oslo). May through August in the far north. Even midsummer synthesis is
      efficient only around midday because sun angle stays modest. Traditional
      summer culture (long outdoor days, beach and lake outings) traps a
      meaningful amount of vitamin D for winter.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Take your vitamin D through the winter.
      The tradition of daily cod liver oil in Nordic countries is grounded in real
      biology, not superstition.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-scandinavia" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
